WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:04.000
Some say it’s an alien, others say a military
experiment.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.250
It’s also been called a dinosaur, a humanoid,
a robot, and even the devil itself.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.980
Earning its name from its favorite prey “the
goatsucker”el chupacabras, stalks the countryside

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.940
and cities alike.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:20.810
Chupacabras are a popular folk legend but
not everyone knows there are two different

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:22.660
versions of the monster.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.160
Reports of el chupacabras occur everywhere
from Puerto Rico, where it actually originated,

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.160
to Maine.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.699
Whenever one is sighted or found dead, the
international press latches on to the story

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:36.010
with the same intensity as the bloodsucker
itself.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.039
Some believe it is a freak of nature.

00:00:38.039 --> 00:00:40.800
Others are convinced it was a culmination
of mass hysteria.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.780
But it’s said to be a monster that doesn’t
normally hunt humans.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.000
So why are we so afraid of it?

00:00:55.899 --> 00:01:01.280
The first reports of el chupacabras are actually
relatively recent as monsters go.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:08.330
It’s a modern monster, first appearing in
1995 in the Puerto Rican town of Canóvanas.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:13.070
There were reports of the deaths of “dozens”
of farm animals whose blood had apparently

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:17.010
been sucked dry by small perforations in the
neck and abdomen.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.820
It caused immediate panic.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.200
No animal was safe.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:25.620
Ducks, rabbits, chickens, pigs, and yes goats,
were all said to have been killed by a grotesque

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:26.620
creature.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.290
In Puerto Rico, chupacabras are said to be
three to five feet tall with wings like a

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:37.140
bat, fangs, a hunched back, large black or
red eyes, and covered with either scales or

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.140
quills.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:43.140
Some reports include tiny arms and the ability
to jump great distances.Others claim it has

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.950
spikes along its back and a long tongue.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:51.490
For the residents of Canóvanas in 1995 this
terrifying monster was a real threat.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:55.460
Their livestock were dying and people were
so scared, that guards were positioned to

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:00.140
protect what animals were left, children were
escorted to school, and some families even

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.080
abandoned their homes.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:07.370
As local and national papers picked up the
story, reports increased across the island.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:14.000
By April 1996, more than 2,000 animals had
fallen victim to el chupacabras.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.530
At different times, Puerto Rico housed U.S.
military personnel.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.730
This includes Fort Buchanan located less than
40 minutes from Canóvanas.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:26.690
Rumors swirled that el chupacabras was an
escaped military experiment.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.340
This is when things get more interesting.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.470
Annddd political.

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:35.810
Mayor José Ramón “Chemo” Soto, who was
up for reelection in 1996, decided to take

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:41.760
matters into his own hands and protect the
people and animals of his town with a safari.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:48.140
In October 1995, Soto organized 200 people,
including armed cops, and set out and catch

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:49.260
the monster.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.120
Dude literally dragged a goat around in a
cage and carried a large crucifix.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.880
The press dubbed him “Chemo Jones”
Soto also loved to give interviews, and when

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.940
the international, English-speaking press
heard of el chupacabras in late 1995, his

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:10.350
quotes appeared in The Washington Post, The
New York Times, and this fun one.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:13.930
Soto wasn’t the only politician to address
el chupacabras.

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.440
The Puerto Rican government decided to step
in.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.819
They were not only concerned for the people
and their livelihoods; they also recognized

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:24.270
the negative impact the monster could have
on tourism.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.490
Biologists were brought in to find the scientific
explanation for the attacks.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:36.250
Theories ranged from bats driven by drought
to seek new food sources, roving packs of

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:42.550
wild dogs, to escaped rhesus monkeys that
had been imported for scientific testing.

00:03:42.550 --> 00:03:47.000
One zoologist believed the majority of the
attacks were caused by a spike in the mongoose

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.000
population.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.770
Don’t let their cuteness fool you, those
things are nuts.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.620
Fun fact: Even though Puerto Ricans are citizens
of the United States and must obey all of

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:00.610
its laws, they don’t have the right to vote
in the presidential election.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:04.900
They’re asked to obey federal laws, but
have little power to change them.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.580
Technically, Puerto Rico is a commonwealth
of the United States.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.849
So Puerto Ricans — at least based on their
lack of voting rights — are outsiders in

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:14.360
their own country.

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:16.519
Which…just seems crazy to me.

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:18.410
What is this, Victorian England?

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:23.850
Despite all of this, or perhaps because of
it, El Chupacabras became a pop culture phenomenon.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:29.180
For some Puerto Ricans, the monster became
an anti-hero, a monstrous symbol for their

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.470
own otherness in relation to the United States.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:37.430
By the end of 1995, thousands of shirts were
printed with illustrations of chupacabras.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:46.690
It inspired three songs, and a cocktail.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.240
In the early 2000s, a different kind of chupacabra

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:51.240
emerged.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.880
Reports from Mexico and the southwestern United
States described el chupacabras as a hairless,

00:04:55.880 --> 00:05:01.560
gaunt animal walking on four legs—a vast
departure from the original Puerto Rican monster.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:06.040
Unlike the Puerto Rican version, actual corpses
have been found.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:12.300
Upon examination, these canine-looking creatures
always turn out to be coyotes, dogs, or raccoons

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.190
with genetic abnormalities or victims of mange.

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:19.669
An animal infected with mange has severe itching
and hair loss.

00:05:19.669 --> 00:05:23.220
Some even have self-inflicted wounds from
scratching and chewing.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:28.080
In advanced cases, the skin of the animal
will become thicker and darker in color, and

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.370
their body may be emaciated.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.350
All of these symptoms can explain the chupacabras
appearance.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:38.630
Because this “chupacabra” is based on
real world animals, it carries less cultural

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:41.350
significance than it’s Puerto Rican cousin.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:46.919
The unique political status of Puerto Rico
helped to shape the el chupacabras myth.

00:05:46.919 --> 00:05:51.780
It became a symbol of cultural otherness,
and the mystery surrounding its origin emphasized

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.430
how those in power can control information.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:58.840
One thing both myths have in common, is that
real animals are actually dying.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:03.770
For farmers and rural citizens, the threat
to their livelihoods is a huge, very real

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:04.770
problem.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:08.660
Monster mythologies are typically thought
of as ancient tales told long ago.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.520
But el chupacabras shows us that these stories
can arise today.

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.260
They highlight our fascination with trying
to explain the unexplained, and allow us to

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.010
tell our cultural histories by merging fact
and fiction.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:24.710
I can’t help but wonder, what form will
the chupacabras take next?

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.100
Have you ever seen el chupacabras?

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:29.490
Let me know in the comments.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:32.340
And don’t forget to subscribe!

